            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The U.S. Constitution requires an actual count of the population for the census."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Thomas R. Lee, Esq.",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "3e34a1134a68630cd60843abd91cdc687d7325c4b75f203b65186dc91b5d8c65",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-3e34a1134a68-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 4,
              "start": 4
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "In the 2000 Census, the Census Bureau added about 1.2\u202fmillion people (0.4\u202f% of the total) by statistically imputing occupants for 620,000 addresses based on neighboring households."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Dellinger",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "3e34a1134a68630cd60843abd91cdc687d7325c4b75f203b65186dc91b5d8c65",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-3e34a1134a68-0002-0005"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 43,
              "start": 43
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Dellinger, utah argues that counting zero people for a known occupied address is a counter\u2011factual enumeration and violates the Constitution\u2019s requirement for an actual count."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Kennedy and Justice Ginsburg",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "3e34a1134a68630cd60843abd91cdc687d7325c4b75f203b65186dc91b5d8c65",
            "evidence_ids": [
